WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:05.300
I think what inspires me is a mix of
the unknown

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.420
and the excitement of something new.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.720
My research focus falls in
the back-and-forth between

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:21.340
digital and physical. Looking into how
physiological information and like brain

00:00:21.359 --> 00:00:26.519
signals electrodermal activity and any
type of physiological sensor can be used

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.680
to allow us to express in the digital
world our needs that we have.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.940
A lot of my work uses Dakota

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.720
motifs and symbols to

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:41.840
show the philosophy of how we as Dakota people approach the world.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.480
Since coming to MIT, 
I've had opportunities to learn

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:52.770
new technologies. I created a piece
specifically for this show that is a

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:57.760
sound vessel. And it's a clay vessel that
actually transmits sound.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:00.540
My work is mostly oil painting.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.400
One of the things
I've shown here

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.340
is my subway series.

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:08.300
It's just basically a series of
snapshots of scenes from my everyday

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:12.930
commute on the Red Line. Mostly
interested in that intersection of these

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.920
personal moments within a very public
space, and kind of

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.380
how they interfere with each other.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:22.900
I view my work as a
socio-technical synthesis.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.060
I'm using geometry and

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:29.880
exploring geometry with the
human body and also trying to evoke

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:35.640
some emotion and sort of Gestalt shifts to
how we actually view reality.

